The o200k_base encoding file -- NOT contained here, fetched after installation
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
File:      src/task_generator/data/o200k_base.tiktoken (3,613,922 bytes)
Status:    NOT in this repository, NOT in the sdist and NOT in the wheel. It is
           git-ignored and excluded from both build targets. It is fetched once, after
           installation, by `python -m task_generator.fetch_encoding`, which reads the
           URL and the expected digest out of the installed tiktoken and downloads
           through tiktoken's own reader. Nothing is redistributed here.
What:      The byte-pair-encoding merge ranks of the o200k_base encoding, in tiktoken's
           file format. The reference tokenizer reads the fetched copy, so that it never
           reaches the network and leaves no byte in any cache directory.
Source:    https://openaipublic.blob.core.windows.net/encodings/o200k_base.tiktoken
           This is the URL that tiktoken 0.14.0 uses for the encoding
           (tiktoken_ext/openai_public.py, function o200k_base). A HEAD request on
           2026-09-21 returned HTTP 200 and a Content-Length equal to the size above.
Integrity: sha256 446a9538cb6c348e3516120d7c08b09f57c36495e2acfffe59a5bf8b0cfb1a2d,
           equal to the expected_hash that tiktoken 0.14.0 pins for it. The fetch
           refuses any other digest, and src/task_generator/tokenizer.py refuses to load
           a file whose digest differs.
License:   NONE FOUND, re-checked on 2026-09-21 and recorded below. The terms under
           which this file may be redistributed could not be established, so it is no
           longer copied into this repository. This NOTICE asserts no terms for it.

           What was checked, and what each showed:

           * The tiktoken 0.14.0 distribution ships one license file, its own MIT
             License (quoted in section 2). That is the license of the tiktoken
             software. The distribution contains no encoding data at all -- no
             .tiktoken file appears in its RECORD -- so the MIT grant has no encoding
             file to cover.
           * The openai/tiktoken source tree (read 2026-09-21) contains no encoding
             data either, and its only license statement outside the LICENSE file is
             pyproject.toml's `license = { file = "LICENSE" }`. Nothing in the tree
             says anything about the hosted files.
           * The source URL returns the file with no license header and no link to
             terms of use.
           * The blob container that serves it holds exactly five files: the four
             encodings and a 92-byte README.md, whose whole text is "# Vocabularies for
             encodings / See https://github.com/openai/tiktoken / Have a wonderful
             day!". It points at the repository; it grants nothing, and the repository
             it points at does not contain the file.

           A pointer from the data to a repository whose license covers that
           repository's software is not a license for the data. Reading it as one would
           be a guess, and this file does not make it. The measurements behind the four
           points above are filed in the vault, under
           projects/task-generator/evidence/tg-publish-prep/encoding-licence/.

Code copied from tiktoken
-------------------------
File:      src/task_generator/tokenizer.py
What:      The pre-tokenization regular expression (_PAT_STR) and the two special-token
           ids (_SPECIAL_TOKENS) are copied from the o200k_base definition in tiktoken
           0.14.0 (tiktoken_ext/openai_public.py), reflowed to this repository's line
           length. The module's own comments mark these two as the copied parts.
License:   tiktoken's MIT License, quoted in section 2.

The vocabulary table
--------------------
File:      src/task_generator/data/vocabulary-v1.json
What:      Written for this repository and released under its MIT License. No
           third-party word list, corpus or dataset is recorded as a source for it.
           Its two 256-word record-id lists are common English words chosen so that each
           is a single o200k_base token inside a record id.
Checked:   On 2026-09-21 the two lists were tested against six public word lists --
           google-10000-english and its no-swears variant, that project's 20k list, the
           EFF large wordlist, dwyl/english-words (words_alpha, 370,105 words) and macOS
           /usr/share/dict/words (web2). None of the six produces the 512 words under
           this table's own selection rule, and two of them cannot even contain them:
           fourteen of the 512 are absent from the 10,000-word list and three are absent
           from the 20,000-word one. What the 512 are drawn from is the o200k_base
           vocabulary under this table's single-token rule, which no English word list
           satisfies. That no public list was ever consulted is not a thing this check
           can establish, and this NOTICE does not claim it: it claims only that none is
           recorded as a source and that none of the obvious candidates can be one. The
           measurements, the candidate lists and the scripts are in the vault, under
           projects/task-generator/evidence/tg-publish-prep/word-lists/.

Model weights and datasets
--------------------------
None are contained in this repository, and no code in it loads or downloads any. Model
names appear in the tests only as strings, for example as a revision-string format and as
tool-call marker names; nothing is fetched.

